  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nLow pressure will move northeast through the Great Lakes region this \nevening. A warm front will lift northeast across the local forecast \narea this evening into the overnight hours. Showers with isolated \nthunderstorms are expected to enter the area from the west with the \nwarm front this evening. There will be minimal instability (~500 \nJ/kg) but plenty of bulk shear given a strong low level jet of 40-50 \nknots with the warm front. The potential for an isolated strong \nstorm capable of producing a localized 50 MPH or greater wind gust, \nparticularly across Northwest and North Central Ohio. SPC has \nmaintained this area in their SWODY1 outlook. The potential for any \nstrong to severe storm will diminish through the overnight hours as \ninstability wanes. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nA cold front will glide east through the morning hours on Friday as \nthe aforementioned low continues to lift northeast across the Lower \nGreat Lakes. Latest hi-res guidance show a line of showers and \nthunderstorms redeveloping east of I-71 after sunrise on Friday \nalong and ahead of the cold front. The line of showers and scattered \nstorms will exit to the east across noon on Friday but expect for \nlingering lake enhanced showers to linger through Friday night \ndownwind of the lake as a surface trough drapes itself across the \nlocal area. Overall rainfall amounts with range between 0.25-0.50\" \nwith the highest amounts confined to Northwest Pennsylvania and the \nCleveland metro given the longer duration lake enhanced rainfall \nwith a Lake Huron connection. Locally higher rainfall possible in \nany thunderstorm. \n\nIn addition to rainfall, winds and gusts will increase with the \nfrontal passage in the CAA regime. Strong southwesterly winds will \npeak at 15-20 MPH with gusts up to 25-30 MPH in the early morning \nhours on Friday. The strongest winds are expected across Northwest \nOhio and along the lakeshore. Winds will turn northerly by Friday \nnight but remain elevated before high pressure builds overhead on \nSaturday and winds decrease. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nA much cooler airmass will be in place behind the cold front as \ntemperatures Friday night into Saturday morning fall into the mid \n30s. The frost potential remains limited given strong wind speeds, \nlingering rain showers and persistent cloud cover. By Saturday \nnight, lows fall into the low 30s across much of the forecast area \nwith a mainly clear sky under high pressure. The probabilities for \nfrost remain high outside of locations near the lakeshore given the \nrelatively warm waters.",
